itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 347, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 90, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The Teachers Insurance and Annuity Association of America (TIAA) is a major provider of insurance and retirement-savings accounts, especially to people working in academia, education, and state and local government. Founded by Andrew Carnegie in 1918, the company has about 5 million customers nationwide and $1.4 trillion in assets under management. Revenue sagged by 2.6% at TIAA in 2021, but profits grew robustly, and the company announced plans to add as many as 2,000 new jobs at a new corporate center in Frisco, Texas.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 285, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Revenues for the financial services provider reached $ 41.6 billion in 2020, up almost 3% from the year prior. Amid the coronavirus pandemic, TIAA offered a voluntary buyout to 75% of its 16,500 employees worldwide; in July 2020, the company reported that the offer resulted in a 10% reduction in its workforce. Then\u2013president and CEO Roger Ferguson Jr. said that TIAA would not lay off any employees for the remainder of 2020 or in 2021. Meanwhile, the company has broken new ground in diversity and culture: This May, Thasunda Brown Duckett succeeded Ferguson as CEO, marking TIAA as the first Fortune 500 company in history to have two Black chief executives in a row.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:596,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:603}\">Revenues for the financial services provider reached $ 41.6 billion in 2020, up almost 3% from the year prior. Amid the coronavirus pandemic, TIAA offered a voluntary buyout to 75% of its 16,500 employees worldwide; in July 2020, the company reported that the offer resulted in a 10% reduction in its workforce. Then\u2013president and CEO Roger Ferguson Jr. said that TIAA would not lay off any employees for the remainder of 2020 or in 2021. Meanwhile, the company has broken new ground in diversity and culture: This May, Thasunda Brown Duckett succeeded Ferguson as CEO, marking TIAA as the first Fortune 500 company in history to have two Black chief executives in a row.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 79, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Revenues for the financial services provider reached $ 41.6 billion in 2020, up almost 3% from the year prior. Amid the coronavirus pandemic, TIAA offered a voluntary buyout to 75% of its 16,500 employees worldwide; in July 2020, the company reported that the offer resulted in a 10% reduction in its workforce. Then\u2013president and CEO Roger Ferguson Jr. said that TIAA would not lay off any employees for the remainder of 2020 or in 2021. Meanwhile, the company has broken new ground in diversity and culture: This May, Thasunda Brown Duckett succeeded Ferguson as CEO, marking TIAA as the first Fortune 500 company in history to have two Black chief executives in a row.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:596,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:603}\">Revenues for the financial services provider reached $ 41.6 billion in 2020, up almost 3% from the year prior. Amid the coronavirus pandemic, TIAA offered a voluntary buyout to 75% of its 16,500 employees worldwide; in July 2020, the company reported that the offer resulted in a 10% reduction in its workforce. Then\u2013president and CEO Roger Ferguson Jr. said that TIAA would not lay off any employees for the remainder of 2020 or in 2021. Meanwhile, the company has broken new ground in diversity and culture: This May, Thasunda Brown Duckett succeeded Ferguson as CEO, marking TIAA as the first Fortune 500 company in history to have two Black chief executives in a row.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 306, "itemDescription": "The financial services provider had $1.1 trillion in assets under management at the end of 2019, up from just shy of $1 trillion the year prior. The year saw TIAA make a major pivot away from retail mortgage lending toward digital mortgages, a change of direction after a 2017 acquisition of EverBank that aimed to expand TIAA's retail footprint. The shift, amid rising interest rates and falling consumer demand, has meant closing some retail branches, with an agreement for U.S. Bank to acquire some."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 81, "itemDescription": "The financial services provider had $1.1 trillion in assets under management at the end of 2019, up from just shy of $1 trillion the year prior. The year saw TIAA make a major pivot away from retail mortgage lending toward digital mortgages, a change of direction after a 2017 acquisition of EverBank that aimed to expand TIAA's retail footprint. The shift, amid rising interest rates and falling consumer demand, has meant closing some retail branches, with an agreement for U.S. Bank to acquire some."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "Worl